Looking at the columns against the causal attribution claim. The outcome is `handling_time_gap` (resolution time differences). The query names channel and priority as the factors of interest (candidate treatments). The added columns describe issue characteristics.

```json
{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "handling_time_gap",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Issue category plausibly drives both which channel/priority a ticket enters through (e.g. security_incident routed as high priority) and how long handling takes, making it a common cause not mentioned in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "system_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Scope (platform_wide vs single_user) plausibly affects priority assignment (broad-impact incidents escalated) and directly lengthens handling time, a genuine common cause surfaced from data."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_path",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The resolution path (code_fix, infrastructure_escalation) is chosen after routing and lies on the causal path from treatment/issue to handling time; it is a mechanism through which time is incurred, so adjusting for it would be a mistake."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_complexity",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Complexity plausibly influences the assigned priority (complex issues prioritised) and independently increases handling time; a genuine unobserved common cause not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "blocking_factor",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Blocking factors (awaiting_deploy, awaiting_customer_action) arise during handling and are the immediate mechanism prolonging time; they sit on the causal path rather than being an antecedent common cause."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["channel", "priority"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "issue_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.8},
    {"name": "system_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.75},
    {"name": "issue_complexity", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.8}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The treatments (channel, priority) come from the original table and are named in the query. The system surfaced several new variables from the issue text. Three of them (issue_category, system_scope, issue_complexity) are plausible common causes of both routing/priority and handling time, none named in the query, demonstrating genuine confounder discovery. However two columns (resolution_path, blocking_factor) are mediators/post-treatment mechanisms on the causal path to handling time; treating them as adjustment variables would bias the estimate, which lowers overall quality. So the set partially realises the causal-attribution claim but mixes in post-treatment variables."
}
```

